if (lcn != SPARSE_LCN64 && lcn + len > sbi->used.bitmap.nbits) {
Reason:
The direct addition `lcn + len` can overflow (wrap around) when both values are large, causing a false negative in the boundary check. This allows LCN ranges that actually exceed the volume to pass validation, potentially leading to out-of-bounds memory access.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (lcn != SPARSE_LCN64) {
    u64 lcn_end;
    if (check_add_overflow(lcn, len, &lcn_end))
        return -EINVAL;
    if (lcn_end > sbi->used.bitmap.nbits) {
        return -EINVAL;
    }
}
Reason:
The fix uses `check_add_overflow()` to safely detect arithmetic overflow before performing the comparison. If overflow occurs, it returns an error immediately; otherwise it compares the correctly computed end LCN against the volume boundary, ensuring only valid ranges are accepted.

